# SSO and OIDC

Wyrd federates two kinds of identity from external OIDC providers: humans who log in through single sign-on, and machine workloads that present a platform-issued JWT. Each tenant configures its own human sign-in as one **tenant OIDC connection** through the API. Workload federation is declared as a trusted issuer with `principal_kind = "workload"` and backs the `jwt-bearer` grant. The two stores never overlap: a human login reads only the tenant's connection, and a `Human` trusted issuer is refused everywhere (TOML boot, `/v1/admin/trusted-issuers`, and `wyrd auth trusted-issuer add`) with `WYRD_AUTH_400_HUMAN_CONNECTION_REQUIRED`.

Workload trusted issuers and bindings are configured as TOML arrays in the same `server.toml` the rest of [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/) uses (`$WYRD_CONFIG_PATH`, `/etc/wyrd/server.toml`, or `~/.config/wyrd/server.toml`). They have no environment-variable form. Any deployment that declares either one must also set `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` so the server knows which tenant they belong to (see [the implicit tenant](/self-hosting/configuration/#the-implicit-tenant)).

## Human single sign-on

A person logs in through the tenant's IdP with the OIDC authorization-code flow, secured by PKCE. Wyrd stores the login state server-side for at most five minutes and verifies the returned ID token, whose audience must be the connection's client id, before issuing its own tokens. The [login flow](/concepts/authentication/#human-oidc-login-flow) in Concepts has the full sequence. OIDC is optional: a server starts, provisions tenants, and serves API keys and workload federation without any connection.

### Deployment setup

Set `WYRD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the origin browsers and IdPs reach Wyrd on, for example `https://wyrd.example.com`. The one callback URL every tenant registers at its IdP is `{WYRD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN}/auth/callback`; it is derived from this setting, never from request headers, and is returned read-only as `callback_url`. Without it, connections cannot be staged or tested. If a connection uses a client secret, the server also needs a [sealing key](/self-hosting/authentication/#sealing-key).

### Connection lifecycle

Every route below takes its tenant from the bearer token and requires `identity_connections:write`, which only the tenant `admin` role holds. `service_accounts:write` alone does not grant it. Each allow or refusal is audited before any provider network call.

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /v1/identity/oidc/connections` | The Active connection and optional Candidate, redacted, plus `callback_url`. |
| `PUT /v1/identity/oidc/candidate` | Create the Candidate, or replace it at the next `revision` (pass `expected_revision`). This is also how you rotate a client secret or group map, even for the same issuer. |
| `POST /v1/identity/oidc/candidate/test` | Probe discovery, issuer, JWKS, the callback URL, and client authentication for the exact `expected_revision`. Success stamps it activatable for 15 minutes. |
| `POST /v1/identity/oidc/candidate/activate` | Promote the tested Candidate and retire the previous Active connection in one transaction. Requires `expected_revision` and a `recovery_api_key`. |
| `POST /v1/identity/oidc/active/deactivate` | Stop human sign-in now. |
| `DELETE /v1/identity/oidc/connections/{id}` | Tombstone a connection: its secret is wiped and its id is kept for history. |

```json
PUT /v1/identity/oidc/candidate
{
  "issuer": "https://idp.example.com/realms/acme",
  "client_id": "wyrd",
  "client_auth": "SecretPost",
  "client_secret": "<secret>",
  "claim_mapping": { "subject": "sub", "email": "email", "groups": "groups" },
  "group_role_map": { "wyrd-admins": ["admin"], "wyrd-writers": ["writer"] },
  "expected_revision": null
}
```

`client_auth` is `SecretBasic`, `SecretPost`, or `Public`; secret methods require `client_secret` and `Public` takes none. `PrivateKeyJwt` is refused with `WYRD_AUTH_400_UNSUPPORTED_CLIENT_AUTH`. A user's roles come only from `group_role_map`; connections have no default roles.

Activation is guarded twice. The Candidate must carry an unexpired test for exactly that revision, so a stale or failed test, or a provider outage, fails closed with `WYRD_AUTH_409_CONNECTION_NOT_TESTED`. And `recovery_api_key` must be a live API key of a headless principal in the same tenant that holds `identity_connections:write`. That proves a way back in exists that does not depend on the new IdP. The key is checked and discarded: it is never stored, logged, or audited. A stale revision, an invalid recovery key, or a competing change returns `WYRD_AUTH_409_CONNECTION_CONFLICT`.

Every replica reads the Active connection from Postgres on each login, so activation, rotation, and deactivation take effect on all replicas without a restart.

### Upgrading from Human trusted issuers

The upgrade migration moves each tenant's existing Human trusted issuer into an Active connection, keeping its claim mapping, group map, JWKS URI, and JWKS TTL. A Human issuer that a workload binding references is kept as a Workload issuer so the binding survives. The migration refuses to run, and leaves the database unchanged, if a tenant has more than one Human issuer, a Human issuer with default roles, an audience different from its client id, `private_key_jwt`, or a secret method with no stored secret. The error names the tenant and the repair to make with the previous release.

## Workload federation

Machine workloads (Kubernetes service accounts, GitHub Actions jobs, GCP Workload Identity, SPIFFE) present a JWT their platform issued. Wyrd verifies the assertion against the issuer's JWKS, then looks up a server-owned binding that names which Service or Agent card the workload represents. The token never names its own card; the binding does.

Two declarations are needed: a trusted issuer with `principal_kind = "workload"`, and a workload binding keyed by `(issuer, subject, audience)`.

```toml
[[trusted_issuers]]
issuer            = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
client_id         = "wyrd"
expected_audience = "wyrd"
client_auth       = "public"
principal_kind    = "workload"

[[workload_bindings]]
issuer   = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
subject  = "repo:acme/deploy:ref:refs/heads/main"
audience = "wyrd"
kind     = "service"
name     = "deploy-svc"
space    = "prod"
version  = "1.0.0"
```

The binding's `kind` must be `service` or `agent`. Any other kind is rejected at boot because it can never resolve a principal. The `subject` is the verified external subject claim, for example a Kubernetes service account (`system:serviceaccount:default/deploy`) or a GitHub Actions repo-and-ref subject.

The workload then exchanges its platform JWT for a Wyrd access token:

```bash
curl -X POST https://<your-server>/auth/token \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "grant_type": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer",
    "assertion": "<platform-issued-jwt>",
    "tenant": "acme"
  }'
```

## Managing workload issuers at runtime

Beyond boot-time TOML, workload trusted issuers and bindings have admin CRUD routes under `/v1/admin/trusted-issuers` and `/v1/admin/workload-bindings` (both require `service_accounts:write`). Use them to add or rotate federation without a server restart. See [Cloud identity](/concepts/cloud-identity/) for the full field reference and request shapes.

## Limitations

- `private_key_jwt` client authentication to the IdP token endpoint is not yet implemented. Use `Public` (PKCE) or a client secret.

